Z||000001||deceler8||11-18-2000||03:06 PM||||Could have been a number of things...most likely metal fatigue due to temp. changes and the effects of salt over the years. <P>Could have been a bad casting from the factory and it finally just went.<P>A rare occurrence nonetheless, for whatever reason.||||reg||1
Z||000002||strdream||11-18-2000||06:48 PM||||I would take a close look at the brake caliper and slide. Specialty cars often set for longer periods of time. Like all winter some times. If the brakes try to sieze up it could be a heat source that can make parts brittle or warped.  <BR>I see you are new and from GR.<BR>Welcome from another new guy from Middleville. You probably never heard of Middleville. Is 20 mi. S.E. of GR.<BR>Ross<BR>   ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-18-2000||09:17 PM||||heat from the rotor should be a non issue to the hub. especially with an AL wheel on. Nothing like 15# of aluminum to aid cooling.<P>I expect the steel was bad. It likely was defective in some way when it was put on. Do you have a picture of it you can post. It's hard to imagine how it broke.||||reg||1
Z||000004||strdream||11-18-2000||10:12 PM||||I wasn't refering to the rotor heat geting to the hub. I was refering to the heat warping the rotor and putting uneven pressure on the hub, causing a similar effect to the improper istallation. I mention the unlikely stuff because everybody knows to check the basics first.  Like an over torqed wheel lug.   ||||reg||1
